\subsection{Surface anomaly localization and detection}
%In addition to the reconstruction and segmentation networks, a light-weight classification network is used that interprets the reconstruction and segmentation outputs to assign an image level anomaly score to an image. The classification network takes the concatenation of the segmentation and recosntruction outputs as an input and is trained as a classification network using a cross entropy loss. The trained network produces accurate anomaly scores without the need for the manual discriminative of the network output which is present in other anomaly detection methods. The classification network is trained in an end-to-end fashion however the gradients are stopped so the classification loss does not influence the training of the reconstruction or segmentation networks.
\ntext{The output of the discriminative sub-network is a pixel-level anomaly detection mask $M_o$, which can be interpreted in a straight-forward way for the image-level anomaly score estimation, i.e., whether an anomaly is present in the image.}
%The pixel-level anomaly detection mask, i.e. the anomaly map $M_o$, is the output of the discriminative sub-network. 
%Thus the image level anomaly score estimation does not require a complex interpretation of the discriminative sub-network output.

First, $M_o$ is smoothed by a mean filter convolution layer to aggregate the local anomaly response information. The final image-level anomaly score $\eta$ is computed by taking the maximum value of the smoothed anomaly score map:
%
\begin{equation}
    \eta = max\big(M_o \ast  f_{s_f \times s_f}\big)\hspace{0.2cm},
    \label{eq:G}
\end{equation}
where $f_{s_f \times s_f}$ is a mean filter of size $s_f \times s_f$ and $\ast$ is the convolution operator. \ntext{In a preliminary study, we trained a classification network for the image-level anomaly classification, but did not observe improvements over the direct score estimation method (\ref{eq:G}).}

%however the results were comparable to the approach of taking the maximum of a smoothed anomaly map as the image-level anomaly score estimation method, which is therefore used in our experiments.

