\subsection{Neural Hill Climbing Performance Analysis}

In this section we compare the performance of NHC to two conventional approaches: Steepest Ascent (SAHC) and Best First (BFHC). We let the algorithms run until they get trapped in a local optima, and repeat the optimization for 2000 different instances.
During the optimization run we computed the gap (\%) to the optimum objective value of the instance and the consumed evaluations.

%In the previous experiment we did not force the model to select an improving move.  However, as previously mentioned, the NHC orders the probability vector to select the first action that improves the solution. For this experiment we will consider a bi-criteria analysis: (1) obtain a high quality solution and (2) reach this solution with the minimum number of solution evaluations. The \textbf{NHC} procedure is guided by the NI model trained with instances of $N=20$ as described in Section \ref{method}. We compare NHC to conventional HC procedures: \textbf{BFHC} and \textbf{SAHC}. We run the algorithms until they get stuck in a local optimum.

